    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_claude` in `app/claude.py`, and both tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed), including a new one.\n\n**It couldn't reuse `build_http_client()` directly.** The pinned anthropic 1.8.0 sends requests through `httpx2`, not `httpx`, and it refuses `httpx` objects with a `TypeError`. So neither `build_http_client()` nor `app.http.TIMEOUT` can be passed to it. Instead, `build_claude` builds an `httpx2` client from what `app/http.py` already defines:\n- `EGRESS_TAG` for the `X-Egress-Tag` header;\n- `log_egress` as the request hook, so every attempt is logged, retries included;\n- `CONNECT_TIMEOUT` and `READ_TIMEOUT`, turned into an `httpx2.Timeout` (`CLAUDE_TIMEOUT`).\n\nA few other details:\n- **Timeout is set on the Anthropic client too.** The SDK attaches its own timeout to every request, which overrides the HTTP client's. Setting it only on the HTTP client wasn't enough, so it's set in both places.\n- **SDK connection defaults are kept.** It uses `anthropic.DefaultHttpxClient`, so the SDK's usual connection limits and redirect handling still apply.\n- **Transport** is passed through only when one is given. `max_retries=2` as requested.\n- **`app/http.py` and `fetch_status` are unchanged.**\n\n**New test:** `tests/test_claude.py` uses an `httpx2.MockTransport` that returns a 529 error once and then a success. It checks that both attempts go through the stub, carry the header and the 5 s / 60 s timeouts, and each produce one `POST https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages` line on `app.egress`.\n\nOne thing to know: whatever transport the tests pass has to be an `httpx2` transport. An `httpx.MockTransport` will be rejected by the SDK."
  },
